 and syria  this is a murderous  lying regime it's an enemy of the united  states and we need to treat it that way  but the chance of this individual ending  up in an american court is pretty remote  well uh for now but we have our ways and  uh we'll see what happens i think it's  important that this uh document this  evidence was unsealed by the court  uh here in washington this morning i  think uh it helps  the citizens of the united states and  really in in the world as a whole see  exactly what the nature of the regime in  tehran is  uh and to think that any commitment they  make would be honored uh is a clear  mistake and i hope in britain and and uh  in the european union that people  realize that  and so you you think it is possible to  you that he could  he could uh face trial in the united  states  what because you'd get a hold of him  somehow or what i mean because or  because of the change in the regime or  what  well you know american law enforcement  is uh is very efficient and as our  cousins to the north and canada say you  know the mounties always get their man  we may get this one  and you were i mean it was no secret an  advocate of maximum pressure on iran and  escalating sanctions and  even  asking i think at one stage for the  military options over the nuclear issue  do you still have that  view  well i've gone beyond that i did before  i was in the white house i think the  only way we're going to get peace and  stability in the middle east is to get a  different regime in tehran  and i have long believed that  their nuclear weapons activities and  their support for terrorist groups like  hamas and hezbollah and the houthis in  yemen  and their direct acts of state terrorism  like going after american citizens on  american soil are all part of the same  mix this is the nature of the regime  this is the regime's ideology this is  the threat we all face and  as i say i hope the information that's  been made public today helps inform uh  citizens so that they know more fully  what the nature of the iranian regime is  and what the threat is i mean but seeing  regime change in iran could lead to all  sorts of  of  difficulties and problems given that  they are you know developing a nuclear  weapon like the ones we have now unlike  the ones we have now  but i mean we saw what happened in iraq  yeah well if if you can imagine a worse  regime than the one we have in tehran  now i'd like to know what it would be  look so the eu  uh is trying to salvage the  the nuclear deal the 2015 iran nuclear  deal you  clearly don't think that's the way  forward  the 2015 deal was a mistake when it was  made the iranians never should have been  allowed any capability to enrich uranium  the deal has not gotten any better with  age the biden administration has spent  close to 18 months on its knees all but  begging iran to let us back in the deal  it's a huge strategic mistake i hope uh  there's a new government coming in  britain i hope britain's view changes  from that of france and germany which  have been mistaken for many many years i  think  people need to wake up to the nature of  this regime they're getting perilously  close to a nuclear weapons capability  which in their hands threatens their  neighbors in the region and really the  the world as a whole  and the potential they would give that  to terrorist groups uh without having to  deliver it via a ballistic missile  should worry all of us how close do you  think they are  well it depends in part on how close  their cooperation with north korea is  since they're both benefiting from the  pakistani nuclear  entrepreneur aq khan who sold them the  initial uranium enrichment technology  who provided them with weapons designs  uh and because we cannot be sure that we  have full intelligence as to what iran  is up to i mean if anybody has learned  anything from prior experience don't  count on knowing what some foreign  government is not doing because  we have been limited in our capabilities  in iran for many years and the idea that  the 2015 deal opened iran's nuclear  program up to real inspection is a total  charade and just on the issue of the the  charges against this individual um  do you still think there's a threat  against you do you  have you  changed your security and are you very  much aware of a threat against you still  well it's not not what my opinion is  it's the the opinion of law enforcement  and the intelligence community i've  been granted the secret service  protection again by president biden  since last december i think  obviously that was the correct decision  i'm grateful that he made that decision  uh and it will continue as long as they  deem it necessary it's not not my call  quick word on donald trump uh this fbi  raid on his  home what did you make of that  well i i don't know what to make of it  because i think there's a lot of  information we don't know and i think a  lot of people on both sides trump's  defenders trump's opponents have gotten  a little hyperthyroid about it  i do think it has obviously generated  considerable attention and i think the  attorney general merrick garland would  be well advised to speak publicly to  this question one one way we could  eliminate some of the uncertainty would  be for donald trump to make public the  search warrant that he was given so that  people can see what the fbi was after  yeah that would be interesting is there  a sense though do you think that this  could help donald trump politically  enable him once again to claim a witch  hunt that it's the establishment against  uh donald trump  sure that's what he's doing now and uh  and that's why i think that you know in  the normal course of things in the  united states as in great britain law  enforcement does not speak when it's  conducting a criminal investigation or  when it's conducting a prosecution it  speaks only in court filings and court  appearances that gives others an  advantage because of tha